## Context
Surfaced by the reading-2 wave integration review (tracker 0578, 2026-06-14).
`make check` failed locally on `test_makefile_delete_on_error` because it
`rglob`s the working tree and flags **untracked** `.claude/worktrees/*` agent
worktree directories (and an `attic/.../Makefile`) as orphaned outputs. These
are not git-tracked and not real Makefile outputs — the test passes on CI
(clean checkout) and on every wave-child merge. This is a pre-existing scanner
overreach, not a wave regression, but it makes local `make check` flaky
whenever agent worktrees are present.

## Relevant files
- `tests/test_makefile_delete_on_error.py` — the scanner; scope its `rglob` to
  git-tracked files only (e.g. `git ls-files`), or exclude `.claude/worktrees/`
  and `attic/`.
- `.gitignore` — optionally add `.claude/worktrees/` if not already ignored.

## Actions
1. Restrict the test's file scan to git-tracked paths (or add explicit
   excludes for `.claude/worktrees/` and `attic/`).
2. Verify it still catches a genuine orphaned tracked output (don't neuter it).

## Test
Add a case (or fixture) proving: an untracked `.claude/worktrees/foo/` dir does
NOT trip the test, while a genuinely orphaned *tracked* output still does.

## Verification
- [ ] `make check` green with agent worktrees present locally
- [ ] The test still fails if a real tracked orphaned output is introduced

## Invariants
- Do not weaken detection of genuine DELETE_ON_ERROR / orphaned-output defects.

## Exit criteria
- `test_makefile_delete_on_error` ignores untracked worktree/attic dirs; still
  catches real orphaned tracked outputs; `make check` green locally.
